<section id="changes"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-summary-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Conversation history now includes tool evidence</h2>
<p>Since the previous site update, Dekopon gained journal replay with bounded summaries and stronger control over shared-channel work. These are the operator-visible changes to review before upgrading.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Changes since the previous site update">
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">What ships</th><th scope="col">What to configure</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Conversation memory</th><td data-label="What ships">Journal records retain tool exchanges and attachment references. Older turns can replay as summaries or metadata, with a notice when detail is omitted.</td><td data-label="What to configure">A journal requires an asset-store path. Set retention and prompt-window bounds; summaries run after the reply and make additional model calls. <a href="/inference/#memory">Memory controls</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Slack and Discord follow</th><td data-label="What ships">An invited agent can follow unmentioned replies on opted-in channel/thread routes. A Slack route can also opt into starting a review from a top-level PR link.</td><td data-label="What to configure">Install the follow provider and grant its capabilities; enable a journal and the route opt-in. Reinstall the Slack app when scopes change. <a href="{{ source }}/docs/gatewayd.md#slack-thread-follow">Follow contract ↗</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Channel and GitHub permissions</th><td data-label="What ships">A mapped channel constrains each sender's grants. GitHub webhook turns carry typed event facts into Cedar, and author-set facts can only deny.</td><td data-label="What to configure">Map the space or repository explicitly and write its policy. A post or an event payload never grants authority. <a href="/guides/access/">Identity and access</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">OpenRouter checks</th><td data-label="What ships">Explicit model probes check tool support before a configuration change. Model traces record OpenRouter's reported cost, and cache hints cover replayed prefixes.</td><td data-label="What to configure"><code>check --probe</code> makes a paid canary call. Keep a dollar limit on the OpenRouter key; local token budgets don't cap dollars. <a href="/inference/">Models and budgets</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Operator console</th><td data-label="What ships"><code>dekopon-console</code> ships in the same release archives and Linux image as both daemons.</td><td data-label="What to configure">Keep the console at the daemon version. Homebrew retains its separate console formula. OpenRouter is not wired into this optional client. <a href="/deploy/#configure">Installation</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Local execution</th><td data-label="What ships">The shell gains an <code>awk</code> subset and glob patterns in <code>case</code>. Runtime thread pools are configurable, and active turns share their shell-thread ceiling with jobs and memory work.</td><td data-label="What to configure">Unsupported awk constructs are refused. Size thread pools for the host; those ceilings do not turn the interpreter into a host shell. <a href="/guides/runtime/">Runtime bounds</a>.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
</section>

<section id="migration"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-details-title" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-path-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Back up state before changing both daemons</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Keep the binaries together.</strong> Upgrade <code>dekopon-brokerd</code>, <code>dekopon-gatewayd</code> and any console together. Preserve the previous binaries, configuration and provider lock. Stop the gateway before the broker; start the broker first and verify the real gateway afterward.</li>
<li><strong>Check the intervening configuration changes.</strong> OTLP now uses HTTP/protobuf only; replace <code>telemetry.transport: grpc</code> and use the receiver's HTTP endpoint. Journal configurations require <code>sessions.assets.path</code>, and removed memory keys must be replaced before startup. Run both configuration checks as the owning service users.</li>
<li><strong>Expect a memory reset when crossing old formats.</strong> Journal v3 resets older-format records at startup. This is not an automatic history migration; reverting a binary cannot restore discarded state. Back up the matching state and review the release-specific instructions before upgrading.</li>
</ol>
<p>Earlier installations also need the <code>dekopond</code> rename and <code>dekopon.dev/gatewayd/v1alpha1</code> configuration API. Brokers accept the <code>provider@0.4.0</code> stream contract and refuse older <code>provider@0.3.0</code> components. By default, managed sets load from a compatible compiled cache: sync populates it, and <code>dekopon-brokerd provider precompile</code> fills or repairs it after an engine change.</p>
<p>The <a href="{{ source }}/docs/upgrading.md">release-pinned upgrade guide ↗</a> gives each breaking change and its rollback limits. Use the <a href="/deploy/mac/">Mac guide</a> or <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#upgrade">Kubernetes checklist</a> for the process and storage layout.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-scope-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Summaries spend outside the local token budget</h2>
<p>Journal-recall routes enable background summaries by default. Those model calls are recorded but not refused by the gateway token governor; set <code>summaries: off</code> for a minimal budgeted setup. Ordinary token windows also reset on restart unless best-effort telemetry restore is configured. Use the model provider's spending controls.</p>
<p>Detached jobs remain opt-in through <code>limits.jobTimeoutMs</code>. Provider child scripts use <code>dekopon:spawn</code> under the caller's shared limits. Neither feature gives a provider access to the Mac's host shell; the optional VM runner is a separate Linux/KVM service.</p>
<p>Dekopon is pre-1.0. Cedar checks declared access, not intent. A permitted endpoint receives its credential, and telemetry retention remains your responsibility. Journal replay restores conversation data; it does not resume a paid effect or undo one that already ran.</p>
</section>

